UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 1, Lesson 2: Drawing with different lines', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you move. Pressing lightly makes a thin, pale line and pressing harder makes a thick, dark one. Wavy lines suggest water or hair, zigzags suggest lightning or spikes, and hatching, a set of short parallel lines, can fill an area to look darker. Hold the pencil loosely, relax your shoulder and draw from your arm, not just your fingers.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou want to draw a calm lake with a thundercloud above it. Choose a line type for the water and a different one for the cloud, and say how you would change the pressure for each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet, and the hard pressure makes the cloud feel heavy and stormy.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists find effects, and rubbing out too early stops you learning.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFill a page with a line chart: draw six boxes and fill each with a different line (thick, thin, wavy, zigzag, hatching, spiral). Then draw a rainy street using at least three of your lines, and label where you used each and what it shows.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"A drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you move. Pressing lightly makes a thin, pale line and pressing harder makes a thick, dark one. Wavy lines suggest water or hair, zigzags suggest lightning or spikes, and hatching, a set of short parallel lines, can fill an area to look darker. Hold the pencil loosely, relax your shoulder and draw from your arm, not just your fingers.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Fill a page with a line chart: draw six boxes and fill each with a different line (thick, thin, wavy, zigzag, hatching, spiral). Then draw a rainy street using at least three of your lines, and label where you used each  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet, and the hard pressure makes the cloud… Reasoning: I used thick and thin by attending to a wavy line rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists… Correction: Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet,… Why: the correction restores accurate use of thick and thin.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists find effects, and rubbing out too early stops you learning. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating thick and thin on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with thick and thin, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet,… This matches the lesson skill for thick and thin.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet,…","Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists…"],"question":"You want to draw a calm lake with a thundercloud above it. Choose a line type for the water and a different one for the cloud, and say how you would change the pressure for each. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists… That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists…","A drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you move. Pressing lightly makes a thin, pale line…","Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet,…","Careful use of a wavy line with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising thick and thin?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a wavy line, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a wavy line used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"A learner is practising thick and thin. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet,… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet,…","Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about thick and thin?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists…","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about thick and thin.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a wavy line?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: to draw a hedgehog, Jin uses short quick zigzag lines for the spikes and lots of hatching on its back to make it darker. Then she…","Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists…","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists…","Fill a page with a line chart: draw six boxes and fill each with a different line (thick, thin, wavy, zigzag, hatching, spiral). Then draw a rainy…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of thick and thin?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating thick and thin on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet, and the hard pressure makes the cloud… Reasoning: I used thick and thin by attending to a wavy line rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with thick and thin, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists… Correction: Strong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet,… Why: the correction restores accurate use of thick and thin.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise thick and thin.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use thick and thin with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a wavy line and explain why it matters.","I can practise a hatching line independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a pencil grip needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you move. Pressing lightly makes a thin, pale line and pressing harder makes a thick, dark one. Wavy lines suggest water or hair, zigzags suggest lightning or spikes, and…","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you move. Pressing lightly makes a thin, pale line and pressing harder makes a thick, dark one. Wavy lines suggest water or hair, zigzags suggest lightning or spikes, and hatching, a set of short parallel lines, can fill an area to look darker. Hold the pencil loosely, relax your shoulder and draw from your arm, not just your fingers."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about thick and thin?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: to draw a hedgehog, Jin uses short quick zigzag lines for the spikes and lots of hatching on its back to make it darker. Then she uses a thin, light curved line for the nose and one thick dark dot for the eye. The different lines show soft and spiky parts in one picture.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou want to draw a calm lake with a thundercloud above it. Choose a line type for the water and a different one for the cloud, and say how you would change the pressure for each.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet, and the hard pressure makes the cloud feel heavy and stormy.","explain":"A drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you move. Pressing lightly makes a thin, pale line and pressing harder makes a thick, dark one. Wavy lines suggest water or hair, zigzags suggest lightning or spikes, and hatching, a set of short parallel lines, can fill an area to look darker. Hold the pencil loosely, relax your shoulder and draw from your arm, not just your fingers.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists find effects, and rubbing out too early stops you learning. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Pencil pressure changes line weight.","caption":"Press to change the line.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Light pressure</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">thin, pale water line</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Hard pressure</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">thick, dark cloud line</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: to draw a hedgehog, Jin uses short quick zigzag lines for the spikes and lots of hatching on its back to make i Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: to draw a hedgehog, Jin uses short quick zigzag lines for the spikes and lots of hatching on its back to make i Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about thick and thin?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: to draw a hedgehog, Jin uses short quick zigzag lines for the spikes and lots of hatching on its back to make it darker. Then she uses a thin, light curved line for the nose and one thick dark dot for the eye. The different lines show soft and spiky parts in one picture. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Hatching and zigzag lines.","caption":"Pick a line for the job.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Hatching</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">short parallel lines, darker</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Zigzag</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">spikes and lightning</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about thick and thin?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists find effects, and rubbing out too early stops you learning. Here is a more helpful example: Example: to draw a hedgehog, Jin uses short quick zigzag lines for the spikes and lots of hatching on its back to make it darker. Then she uses a thin, light curved line for the nose and one thick dark dot for the eye. The different lines show soft and spiky parts in one picture.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.'' The soft pressure suggests quiet, and the hard pressure makes the cloud feel heavy and stormy.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists find effects, and rubbing out too early stops you learning. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about thick and thin?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you move. Pressing lightly makes a thin, pale line and pressing harder makes a thick, dark one. Wavy lines suggest water or hair, zigzags suggest lightning or spikes, and hatching, a set of short parallel lines, can fill an area to look darker. Hold the pencil loosely, relax your shoulder and draw from your arm, not just your fingers. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 1, Lesson 2: Drawing with different lines","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Drawing, line, shape and colour","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: to draw a hedgehog, Jin uses short quick zigzag lines for the spikes and lots of hatching on its back to make it darker. Then she uses a thin, light curved line for the nose and one thick dark dot for the eye. The different lines show soft and spiky parts in one picture.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Pencil pressure changes line weight.","caption":"Press to change the line.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Light pressure</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">thin, pale water line</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Hard pressure</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">thick, dark cloud line</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Hatching and zigzag lines.","caption":"Pick a line for the job.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Hatching</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">short parallel lines, darker</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Zigzag</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">spikes and lightning</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w01-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
